Built from the [Tauri + SvelteKit + Rust template](https://github.com/NeonSupernova/tauri-app): one codebase that
runs as a website and as a desktop/mobile app, with Rust both natively (Tauri) and in the browser (WebAssembly).

## Prerequisites

- [Bun](https://bun.sh/) and Node.js ≥ 22.17
- [Rust](https://rustup.rs/) with the WASM target: `rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown`
- [wasm-pack](https://rustwasm.github.io/wasm-pack/installer/) (`cargo install wasm-pack`)
- Tauri's [system dependencies](https://v2.tauri.app/start/prerequisites/) for your OS

## Commands

```sh
bun install
bun run tdev        # desktop app with hot reload (builds WASM first)
bun run tbuild      # desktop installers in target/release/bundle
bun run wasm-build && bun run dev     # web only, http://localhost:1420
bun run wasm-build && bun run build   # static website in build/
bun run check       # type-check the frontend
bun run test        # Rust unit tests
```

Mobile: `bunx tauri android init` / `bunx tauri ios init`, then `bunx tauri android dev` / `bunx tauri ios dev`.

## Architecture

```
crates/core   Plain Rust shared by both targets (QR codes, pixel renderer, your logic)
   ├── src-tauri   Native backend: Tauri commands + plugins (OS APIs). Desktop/mobile only.
   └── wasm        wasm-bindgen wrappers → src/lib/wasm (generated). Runs in any browser.
src/lib/platform.ts  The only place that picks native vs WASM/web. Components call it.
src/routes      SvelteKit pages (prerendered with adapter-static)
```

### Adding a capability

1. Write the logic once in `crates/core` (no platform APIs; add a unit test).
2. Expose it natively: a `#[tauri::command]` in `src-tauri/src/lib.rs`, registered in `generate_handler!`.
3. Expose it to the browser: a `#[wasm_bindgen]` function in `wasm/src/lib.rs`.
4. Add a function to `src/lib/platform.ts` that calls `invoke()` when `native`, else the WASM export.

For OS-only features (files, notifications, …) add a [Tauri plugin](https://v2.tauri.app/plugin/), grant its
permission in `src-tauri/capabilities/default.json`, and give `platform.ts` a web fallback (or hide the feature).

### When to use WASM vs native vs JS

- **WASM** pays off for heavy, self-contained compute: pixels, simulations, parsing, compression, crypto. Keep
  JS↔WASM calls coarse (one call per frame/job) and share buffers through WASM memory like
  `src/routes/graphics` does.
- **Native (Tauri command)** is fastest of all and can use threads and the OS, but only exists in the app.
- **Plain JS** is fine for small jobs; crossing into WASM for tiny work can cost more than it saves.

## Notes

- Import from `src/lib` with `#lib/...` (Node subpath imports, configured in `package.json`), including the
  file extension, e.g. `#lib/platform.ts`.
- SvelteKit config lives in `vite.config.ts`, including the Content Security Policy. Add the origins of any APIs you
  call to `connect-src` there and in `src-tauri/tauri.conf.json`.
- `binaryen` is a dev dependency so `wasm-pack` always uses a known-good `wasm-opt`.
